(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-12-06"] = {"6.1":{"s":"For any reaction we can ask three things: how far it goes (equilibrium), whether it can happen (thermodynamics) and how fast it goes. Chemical kinetics is the study of the third question: reaction rates and what controls them.","x":["For a chemical reaction we can ask three different questions. Can it happen at all (thermodynamics: the sign of ΔG)? How far does it go (chemical equilibrium)? And how fast does it go (chemical kinetics)? This chapter answers the third.","Speed matters in life. A medicine must act in a reasonable time, food must not spoil too quickly, and an industrial process must be fast enough to be economical. Some reactions are almost instant (the neutralisation of an acid by a base), some take hours (the rusting of iron) and some take millions of years (the formation of coal).","Kinetics also tells us about the way the reaction takes place, step by step. This is the reaction mechanism. You will learn how to define and measure the rate, how it depends on concentration (the rate law and order), how concentration changes with time, and how temperature and catalysts change the rate."],"e":"Diamond turning into graphite is thermodynamically spontaneous, but so slow that diamonds last for ever. Kinetics explains why spontaneous does not mean fast."},"6.2":{"s":"Rate of reaction is how fast reactants are used up or products formed: the change in concentration per unit time (mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹). Average rate is taken over an interval. Instantaneous rate is the slope of the concentration-time graph at one moment. To get one rate for the whole reaction, divide by the stoichiometric coefficients.","f":[{"x":"rate = -(1/a)*d[A]/dt = (1/c)*d[C]/dt","n":"Rate of aA + bB -> cC + dD: divide each rate of change by its coefficient; minus sign for reactants.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 6.2, p.121"}],"w":["The rate of consumption of B in A + 3B -> 2C is three times that of A; the rate of REACTION is one number."],"x":["The rate of a reaction is how fast the concentration of a reactant falls, or of a product rises, as time goes by. For a reaction A → B, the average rate over a time Δt is −Δ[A]/Δt = +Δ[B]/Δt. The unit is mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹ (or mol L⁻¹ min⁻¹ for slower changes).","The minus sign for a reactant makes the rate positive, since [A] falls. When the coefficients are not all 1, the rate is defined by dividing by the coefficient. For aA + bB → cC + dD: rate = −(1/a) d[A]/dt = −(1/b) d[B]/dt = (1/c) d[C]/dt = (1/d) d[D]/dt. That gives one common rate for the whole reaction.","The rate usually falls as the reaction proceeds, because the reactants get used up. So we distinguish the average rate over an interval from the instantaneous rate at one moment, which is the slope of the tangent to the concentration–time curve at that moment (the limit as Δt tends to zero)."],"e":"In 2N₂O₅ → 4NO₂ + O₂, if N₂O₅ falls at 0.02 mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹, then NO₂ rises at 0.04 and O₂ at 0.01 mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹, and the reaction rate is 0.01."},"6.3":{"s":"The rate law gives rate = k[A]^x[B]^y, where x and y are found by experiment, not from the balanced equation. The order of reaction is x + y. k is the rate constant, and its units depend on the order. Rate depends on concentration; k does not.","f":[{"x":"rate = k*[A]^x*[B]^y","n":"Rate law; x, y found by experiment; overall order = x + y.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 6.3, p.123"}],"w":["Order can be zero or a fraction (CH₃CHO decomposition is 3/2 order).","Units of k change with order: mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹ (zero), s⁻¹ (first), L mol⁻¹ s⁻¹ (second)."],"x":["The rate depends on the concentrations of the reactants. The rate law (rate equation) writes this as rate = k[A]^x[B]^y, where k is the rate constant and the powers x and y are found by experiment. They are not the coefficients in the balanced equation, unless the reaction is a single step.","The order with respect to A is x, the order with respect to B is y, and the overall order is x + y. Orders can be zero, whole numbers or fractions. For zero order the rate does not depend on the concentration; for first order the rate is proportional to the concentration; for second order it goes with the square.","To find the order, compare the initial rates in experiments in which the concentrations are changed one at a time. If doubling [A] doubles the rate, the order in A is 1. If it quadruples the rate, the order is 2. If there is no change, the order is 0.","The unit of k depends on the order: s⁻¹ for first order, mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹ for zero order and L mol⁻¹ s⁻¹ for second order. In general, the unit is (mol/L)^(1−n) s⁻¹ for order n. The rate constant is the rate when all concentrations are 1 mol/L. A large k means a fast reaction. Some reactions are pseudo-first-order: if one reactant is in large excess, its concentration is nearly constant, so the rate seems to depend on the other only."],"e":"Rate = k[A]²[B]: the order is 3. If [A] is doubled and [B] is tripled, the rate changes by 2² × 3 = 12 times."},"6.4":{"s":"An elementary reaction happens in one step. Its molecularity is the number of particles that collide in that step: 1, 2 or rarely 3. Complex reactions go through several elementary steps. The slowest step is the rate-determining step. Order is found by experiment; molecularity comes from the mechanism.","w":["Molecularity is always a whole number (1, 2, 3) and only defined for an elementary step."],"x":["Most reactions do not take place in one step as the equation suggests. They are a sequence of simple steps, the reaction mechanism. Each step is an elementary reaction, which really takes place as written, with the molecules colliding.","The molecularity of an elementary reaction is the number of reactant particles that collide and react in that step. One particle: unimolecular (for example the decomposition of N₂O₄ in one step). Two: bimolecular (the most common). Three: termolecular (rare, since the chance of three particles meeting at once is very small). Molecularity is always a whole number and is never zero or a fraction.","For an elementary step the rate law can be written straight from the equation, so the order equals the molecularity. For a complex reaction this is not true: molecularity has no meaning for the overall reaction, and the order must be measured.","In a multistep mechanism, the slowest step is the rate-determining step. The overall rate law depends on this step, along with any steps before it. Species that are produced and then used up inside the mechanism are intermediates. They do not appear in the overall equation."],"e":"The reaction H₂ + I₂ → 2HI was long thought to be a single bimolecular step, but evidence shows a more complicated mechanism, which shows why a rate law must be tested by experiment."},"6.5":{"s":"Integrated rate laws give concentration against time. Zero order: [A] falls in a straight line, and t½ = [A]₀/(2k). First order: k = (2.303/t) log([A]₀/[A]), and t½ = 0.693/k, the same at any starting concentration. Radioactive decay is first order. A pseudo-first-order reaction behaves as first order because one reactant is in large excess.","f":[{"x":"k = (2.303/t)*log_10([A]_0/[A]_t)","n":"First order integrated rate law.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 6.5, p.126"},{"x":"[A]_t = [A]_0*e^(-k*t)","n":"First order: concentration falls exponentially.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 6.5, p.126"},{"x":"t_(1/2) = 0.693/k","n":"First order half life: independent of the starting concentration.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 6.5, p.127"},{"x":"k = (2.303/t)*log_10(P_i/(2*P_i - P))","n":"First order gas reaction A(g) -> B(g) + C(g); P_i = initial pressure, P = total pressure at time t.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 6.5, p.128"},{"x":"k*t = [A]_0 - [A]_t","n":"Zero order integrated rate law; units of k are mol L^-1 s^-1.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 6.5, p.129"},{"x":"t_(1/2) = [A]_0/(2*k)","n":"Zero order half life: proportional to the starting concentration.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 6.5, p.130"}],"w":["First order: a plot of log[A] against t is a straight line of slope −k/2.303.","Zero order: [A] against t is a straight line of slope −k."],"x":["The integrated rate law tells us the concentration at any time. It is found by integrating the differential rate law.","Zero order (rate = k): [A] = [A]₀ − kt. A plot of [A] against t is a straight line with slope −k. The half-life is t½ = [A]₀/2k, which is proportional to the starting concentration. Zero-order reactions are rare: examples are the decomposition of ammonia on a hot platinum surface (when the surface is saturated), and some enzyme reactions at high substrate concentration.","First order (rate = k[A]): ln[A] = ln[A]₀ − kt, or k = (2.303/t) log([A]₀/[A]). A plot of ln[A] against t is a straight line with slope −k. The half-life is t½ = 0.693/k, a constant that does not depend on the starting concentration. After n half-lives, the fraction left is (1/2)ⁿ. Radioactive decay, and the decomposition of N₂O₅ and H₂O₂ are first order.","These give you a way to find the order from data. Plot [A] against t (a straight line means zero order) or ln[A] against t (a straight line means first order), or check whether the half-life changes with concentration."],"e":"A first-order reaction with k = 0.05 min⁻¹ has t½ = 0.693/0.05 = 13.9 min. After 30 min, the fraction left is e^(−1.5) = 0.223."},"6.6":{"s":"Molecules must collide to react, but most collisions do nothing. A collision works only if the molecules have at least the activation energy (enough to climb the energy barrier) and the right orientation. So the rate is far smaller than the collision rate.","x":["For two molecules to react, they must first meet, so collision is the starting point. But if every collision led to a reaction, all gas reactions would be over in a fraction of a second. At room temperature, a molecule collides about 10¹⁰ times a second, and only a tiny part of these collisions causes a reaction.","Collision theory says that a collision is effective only if two conditions are met. First, the molecules must have enough energy, at least the threshold energy, to break the old bonds. The extra energy the reactants need above their average is the activation energy, E_a. Second, they must collide with the proper orientation, so that the atoms that must bond come close to each other.","The rate is the collision frequency Z, times the fraction of collisions with enough energy, e^(−Ea/RT), times a steric (orientation) factor. At the moment of an effective collision there is a short-lived, high-energy arrangement, the activated complex or transition state, which forms products.","The energy diagram shows reactants, a peak (the transition state) and products. The height from the reactants to the peak is E_a for the forward reaction, and from the products to the peak for the backward reaction. The difference between the reactants and products is ΔH."],"e":"For CH₃Br + OH⁻, if the OH⁻ approaches the carbon from the side of the bromine, the reaction can take place; from the other side, a collision with enough energy still fails."},"6.7":{"s":"Most reactions go faster when heated, often about twice as fast for a 10 °C rise, because many more molecules then have the activation energy. Arrhenius equation: k = A e^(−Ea/RT). Plotting log k against 1/T gives a straight line, from which Ea can be found.","f":[{"x":"k = A*e^(-E_a/(R*T))","n":"Arrhenius equation; A = pre-exponential factor, E_a = activation energy, T in kelvin.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 6.7, p.132"},{"x":"log_10(k) = -E_a/(2.303*R)*(1/T) + log_10(A)","n":"Arrhenius plot: log k against 1/T is a line of slope -E_a/(2.303 R).","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 6.7, p.132"},{"x":"log_10(k_2/k_1) = (E_a/(2.303*R))*((T_2 - T_1)/(T_1*T_2))","n":"Activation energy from rate constants at two temperatures.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 6.7, p.133"}],"w":["Use R = 8.314 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ with the activation energy in joules, not kilojoules."],"x":["Most reactions go faster at a higher temperature. A rule of thumb for reactions near room temperature is that a rise of 10 °C about doubles or triples the rate. The ratio of the rate constants at T + 10 and T is the temperature coefficient, usually between 2 and 3.","The reason is not that the collisions become much more frequent (they rise by only a few per cent), but that the fraction of molecules with energy of at least E_a rises rapidly. Arrhenius expressed this as k = A e^(−Ea/RT), where A is the frequency factor (it depends on the collision rate and orientation) and E_a is the activation energy.","Taking logs: ln k = ln A − E_a/RT. A plot of ln k against 1/T is a straight line with slope −E_a/R, which gives E_a. Comparing two temperatures: ln(k₂/k₁) = (E_a/R)(1/T₁ − 1/T₂), or log(k₂/k₁) = (E_a/2.303R)(T₂ − T₁)/(T₁T₂).","A reaction with a large E_a is more sensitive to temperature. Cooking and the storage of food in a refrigerator are both everyday uses: food spoils more slowly in the cold, since bacterial processes slow down."],"e":"If E_a = 50 kJ/mol, then raising the temperature from 300 K to 310 K multiplies k by exp[(50 000/8.314)(1/300 − 1/310)] = 1.91, nearly double."},"6.8":{"s":"A catalyst speeds up a reaction without being used up. It gives the reaction a different path with a lower activation energy. It does not change ΔH or the position of equilibrium; it only helps equilibrium be reached sooner. Enzymes are biological catalysts.","w":["A catalyst lowers the activation energy of the forward and backward reactions equally, so K does not change."],"x":["A catalyst is a substance that speeds up a reaction and is not used up in it. It takes part in the mechanism, but is regenerated at the end, so it does not appear in the overall equation. Examples: MnO₂ for the decomposition of KClO₃, iron in the Haber process, and enzymes in living cells.","A catalyst works by offering a different route with a lower activation energy. Since the fraction of molecules with enough energy depends exponentially on E_a, even a modest lowering gives a big increase in rate. The energy diagram has a lower peak, but the energies of the reactants and the products are the same.","What a catalyst does not do: it does not change ΔH or ΔG of the reaction, it does not make a non-spontaneous reaction go, and it does not change the equilibrium constant or the position of equilibrium. It speeds up the forward and the backward reactions by the same factor, so equilibrium is reached sooner, but the amounts at equilibrium are unchanged.","Catalysts are used widely: the catalytic converter in a car, hydrogenation of oils with nickel, and the contact process for sulphuric acid with V₂O₅. Catalysts can be specific (an enzyme will work on one substrate only) and can be poisoned by impurities."],"e":"Without a catalyst, a reaction has E_a = 75 kJ/mol. A catalyst lowers it to 50 kJ/mol. At 300 K, the rate rises by about exp(25 000/(8.314 × 300)) = 2 × 10⁴ times."}};
